                'title' => 'NIST 800-88 vs. DoD 5220.22-M: Which Data Destruction Standard Do You Need?',
                'slug' => 'nist-800-88-vs-dod-5220-data-destruction-standards',
                'image_path' => 'blog_images/it-data-destruction.jpg',
                'excerpt' => 'A deep dive into media sanitization protocols, comparing physical degaussing, mechanical shredding, and multi-pass cryptographic erasure.',
                'content_blocks' => json_decode('[{"type": "html", "content": "<h2>Understanding Media Sanitization in the Flash Era</h2><p>For decades, the United States Department of Defense standard (DoD 5220.22-M) was the de facto requirement for magnetic disk sanitization. However, the rise of solid-state drives (SSDs), NVMe media, and wear-leveling NAND controllers has made traditional multi-pass overwriting obsolete and potentially dangerous.</p><h3>Why NIST 800-88 Rev 1 is the Gold Standard</h3><p>The National Institute of Standards and Technology (NIST) 800-88 framework introduces three explicit sanitization tiers:</p><ol><li><strong>Clear:</strong> Logical sanitization using standard user-accessible commands across all writeable storage locations.</li><li><strong>Purge:</strong> Executes controller-level Cryptographic Erase or physical block-level firmware wiping, making target recovery infeasible even using laboratory equipment.</li><li><strong>Destroy:</strong> Mechanical shredding, disintegration, or incineration reducing the physical medium to sub-millimeter particles.</li></ol>"}]', true),
