                'title' => 'Multi-Tenant SaaS Architecture: Database Isolation Patterns and Scaling',
                'slug' => 'multi-tenant-saas-architecture-database-patterns',
                'image_path' => 'blog_images/saas-application.jpg',
                'excerpt' => 'Explore database-per-tenant, schema-per-tenant, and shared-schema designs with row-level security for high-growth SaaS platforms.',
                'content_blocks' => json_decode('[{"type": "html", "content": "<h2>Architectural Decision-Making for Modern Cloud SaaS</h2><p>Engineering multi-tenant applications requires balancing infrastructure cost efficiency against stringent tenant data isolation requirements. Choosing the wrong data storage pattern early in the product lifecycle can lead to painful, expensive refactoring later.</p><h3>The Three Common Multi-Tenancy Patterns</h3><ol><li><strong>Separate Database per Tenant:</strong> Total physical data segregation. Highest security and compliance isolation, simplified backups, but higher idle infrastructure costs.</li><li><strong>Shared Database, Separate Schemas:</strong> Moderate resource utilization with distinct namespace tables per customer.</li><li><strong>Shared Database, Shared Schema with Tenant ID:</strong> Lowest operating cost. Relies on application-level filtering or database Row-Level Security (RLS) policies.</li></ol>"}]', true),
